<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} jsonld={jsonld} navVariant="inner">
<section class="hero">
<div class="wrap">
<span class="eyebrow">Stage 3 — Before payment</span>
<h1>The carton is part of the product</h1>
<p class="lead">For parcel-shipped furniture, the box matters as much as the piece inside. We drop-test, review packaging specs and validate hardware kits so your returns rate stops eating the margin.</p>
<div class="hero-ctas"><a class="btn btn-solid" href="/#book">Book an inspection</a><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/why-independent/">Why independent</a></div>
</div>
</section>
<section><div class="wrap"><div class="sec-head"><div><h2>What packaging validation covers</h2></div></div><div class="grid"><a class="card rv" href="/pre-shipment-inspection-india/"><h3>ISTA 3A drop sequence</h3><p>Sampled cartons through the parcel-network drop protocol—six orientations, photographed results.</p></a><a class="card rv" href="/#book"><h3>Packaging spec review</h3><p>Corner protection, honeycomb board, void fill and carton grade vs the weight actually inside.</p></a><a class="card rv" href="/#book"><h3>Flat-pack kit integrity</h3><p>Hardware BOM counts, instruction sheets, part labelling—the difference between a sale and a return.</p></a><a class="card rv" href="/#book"><h3>Marketplace requirements</h3><p>Packaging expectations for Amazon-class parcel networks checked before goods are sealed.</p></a></div></div></section><section><div class="wrap prose-wrap"><div class="sec-head"><div><h2>Why transit damage is worth testing for</h2></div></div><p class="prose rv">Transit damage is the silent margin killer for e-commerce furniture: the product was perfect, the journey wasn't. A failed drop test before shipment costs a packaging respec; a failed journey after shipment costs return freight, refunds and reviews. Buyers typically add this to a pre-shipment inspection on the same visit—one booking, both risks covered. Priced from $149 when added to a pre-shipment inspection on the same visit, or $249 booked on its own — buyer-paid, fixed at booking.</p></div></section>
<section><div class="wrap prose-wrap"><div class="sec-head"><div><h2>Why the box is part of the product</h2></div></div><p class="prose rv">For furniture that ships as a parcel rather than in a supervised container, the packaging is not an afterthought — it is the last line of defence, and the one most often skipped. A piece can pass every inspection for finish and construction and still arrive cracked, because nobody tested whether the carton survives the parcel network. Transit damage is the silent margin killer for e-commerce furniture: the product was perfect, the journey was not, and the cost lands after you have been paid — as return freight, refunds and one-star reviews about a table that left the factory flawless.</p></div></section><section><div class="wrap"><div class="sec-head"><div><h2>Where transit validation pays off</h2></div></div><div class="grid"><a class="card rv" href="/#book"><h3>Flat-pack and RTA furniture</h3><p>Kit integrity is its own failure mode: a missing bolt or an unlabelled part turns a five-star product into a return. We count the hardware against the BOM and check instruction sheets and part labelling.</p></a><a class="card rv" href="/buyers/ecommerce/"><h3>Marketplace and parcel sellers</h3><p>Amazon, Wayfair and similar carry packaging requirements a container shipper never meets by accident. We validate against them before the run is boxed.</p></a><a class="card rv" href="/#book"><h3>Anything that has been coming back damaged</h3><p>If your returns are clustering on transit damage, a drop test before the next shipment tells you whether the packaging spec is the cause — and a respec is far cheaper than another damaged container.</p></a></div></div></section><section><div class="wrap prose-wrap"><div class="sec-head"><div><h2>How validation runs, and what it costs</h2></div></div><p class="prose rv">The core test is an ISTA 3A drop sequence: sampled cartons are run through the standard parcel-network drop orientations and the results photographed, so you see how your packaging actually behaves, not how it looks. Alongside the drop test we review the packaging specification — corner protection, honeycomb board, void fill and carton grade against the weight actually inside — and, for flat-pack, the kit itself: hardware counts against the BOM, instruction sheets and part labelling. Most buyers add validation to a <a href="/pre-shipment-inspection-india/">pre-shipment inspection</a> on the same visit, from $149, or book it standalone at $249 — buyer-paid, with the report inside 24 hours.</p></div></section><section id="faq">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head"><div><h2>Packaging and transit validation FAQ</h2></div></div>
    <div class="faq">
      <details class="rv"><summary>What is ISTA 3A drop testing?</summary><p>The parcel-network drop protocol. We run sampled cartons through six orientations and photograph the results.</p></details>
      <details class="rv"><summary>What is checked besides the drop test?</summary><p>Packaging spec — corner protection, honeycomb board, void fill and carton grade against the weight actually inside — plus flat-pack kit integrity (hardware BOM counts, instruction sheets, part labelling) and marketplace packaging requirements.</p></details>
      <details class="rv"><summary>Can this be combined with a pre-shipment inspection?</summary><p>Yes. Buyers typically add it to a <a href="/pre-shipment-inspection-india/">pre-shipment inspection</a> on the same visit, so one booking covers both risks.</p></details>
      <details class="rv"><summary>Why test packaging before the container ships?</summary><p>A failed drop test before shipment costs a packaging respec. A failed journey after shipment costs return freight, refunds and reviews.</p></details>
      <details class="rv"><summary>How much does packaging and transit validation cost?</summary><p>From $149 when added to a pre-shipment inspection on the same visit, or $249 booked on its own. The fee is buyer-paid and fixed at booking.</p></details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
